1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,277
Kick a ball, and it flies.

2
00:00:02,277 --> 00:00:04,059
Drop a pen, and it falls.

3
00:00:04,059 --> 00:00:06,686
A magnet holds a note on a steel cupboard.

4
00:00:07,086 --> 00:00:09,383
In two of these, nothing touches.

5
00:00:09,383 --> 00:00:10,483
So what pulls?

6
00:00:10,933 --> 00:00:15,438
Aarav rubs a plastic comb in his dry
hair, and holds it over tiny bits

7
00:00:15,438 --> 00:00:16,338
of paper.

8
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:20,118
The bits jump up to the comb,
before it even touches them.

9
00:00:20,118 --> 00:00:21,909
How can a pull cross a gap?

10
00:00:22,409 --> 00:00:25,906
Let's find out, with contact
and non-contact forces.

11
00:00:26,506 --> 00:00:29,896
Every force is a push or
a pull between two bodies.

12
00:00:30,246 --> 00:00:33,033
Some forces act only while bodies touch.

13
00:00:33,033 --> 00:00:34,849
These are contact forces.

14
00:00:35,249 --> 00:00:36,809
A hand pushing a door.

15
00:00:36,809 --> 00:00:39,732
A table holding up a
book, the normal force.

16
00:00:39,732 --> 00:00:41,482
Friction on a sliding box.

17
00:00:41,482 --> 00:00:43,667
A tight rope's pull, the tension.

18
00:00:44,067 --> 00:00:47,324
Others act across a gap,
with nothing touching.

19
00:00:47,324 --> 00:00:49,260
These are non-contact forces.

20
00:00:49,660 --> 00:00:51,635
Gravity pulls a falling pen.

21
00:00:51,635 --> 00:00:53,255
A magnet pulls a pin.

22
00:00:53,255 --> 00:00:55,705
A charged comb pulls bits of paper.

23
00:00:56,355 --> 00:00:57,938
To the motion notebook.

24
00:00:57,938 --> 00:00:59,877
A hand moves toward a block.

25
00:00:59,877 --> 00:01:02,511
A charged comb moves toward a paper bit.

26
00:01:02,861 --> 00:01:04,405
The hand comes closer.

27
00:01:04,405 --> 00:01:06,543
Ten millimetres away, no force.

28
00:01:06,543 --> 00:01:08,428
One millimetre, still none.

29
00:01:08,828 --> 00:01:09,728
Touch.

30
00:01:09,431 --> 00:01:11,874
Now the push appears, all at once.

31
00:01:11,874 --> 00:01:13,901
A contact force needs contact.

32
00:01:14,302 --> 00:01:15,380
Now the comb.

33
00:01:15,380 --> 00:01:17,887
Forty millimetres away, a tiny pull.

34
00:01:17,887 --> 00:01:19,885
Closer, and the pull grows.

35
00:01:20,285 --> 00:01:24,221
At ten millimetres, the pull beats
the paper's weight, and it jumps.

36
00:01:24,621 --> 00:01:28,642
On the graph, the comb's pull
grows smoothly as the gap shrinks.

37
00:01:28,642 --> 00:01:31,595
The hand's push is zero
until the gap is zero.

38
00:01:31,995 --> 00:01:33,139
Slow-mo replay.

39
00:01:33,139 --> 00:01:35,607
Freeze, just as the paper lifts.

40
00:01:35,957 --> 00:01:37,350
The comb pulls up.

41
00:01:37,350 --> 00:01:38,732
Gravity pulls down.

42
00:01:38,732 --> 00:01:41,518
The pull is bigger, so the paper rises.

43
00:01:42,168 --> 00:01:44,472
Now the key idea, step by step.

44
00:01:44,822 --> 00:01:45,722
Step one.

45
00:01:45,677 --> 00:01:47,980
A force always needs two bodies.

46
00:01:47,980 --> 00:01:49,856
One acts, one feels it.

47
00:01:50,606 --> 00:01:51,506
Step two.

48
00:01:51,408 --> 00:01:52,815
It works both ways.

49
00:01:52,815 --> 00:01:57,449
The comb pulls the paper, and the
paper pulls the comb, just as hard.

50
00:01:58,199 --> 00:01:59,099
Step three.

51
00:01:59,087 --> 00:02:02,957
For a contact force, the pair
acts where the surfaces touch.

52
00:02:03,707 --> 00:02:04,607
Step four.

53
00:02:04,567 --> 00:02:08,124
For a non-contact force, the
pair acts across the gap.

54
00:02:08,124 --> 00:02:09,853
Earth pulls a ball down.

55
00:02:09,853 --> 00:02:11,582
The ball pulls Earth up.

56
00:02:12,332 --> 00:02:13,387
Remember this.

57
00:02:13,387 --> 00:02:17,636
Every force is an interaction
between two bodies, touching or not.

58
00:02:18,336 --> 00:02:20,479
But how does a hand push at all?

59
00:02:20,479 --> 00:02:21,472
Link by link.

60
00:02:21,822 --> 00:02:25,554
Everything is made of atoms,
with electrons on the outside.

61
00:02:25,954 --> 00:02:30,260
When your hand presses a block, the
atoms of the two surfaces come very

62
00:02:30,260 --> 00:02:31,160
close.

63
00:02:31,238 --> 00:02:35,331
Their electrons push each other apart,
with an electric force that grows

64
00:02:35,331 --> 00:02:36,650
as they get closer.

65
00:02:37,050 --> 00:02:38,961
We feel that as a push.

66
00:02:38,961 --> 00:02:43,415
So deep down, contact forces are
electric forces between atoms.

67
00:02:44,065 --> 00:02:45,131
Side by side.

68
00:02:45,131 --> 00:02:47,364
Contact: a hand pushing a book.

69
00:02:47,364 --> 00:02:49,902
Stop touching, and the push is gone.

70
00:02:50,302 --> 00:02:52,914
Non-contact: Earth pulling a falling pen.

71
00:02:52,914 --> 00:02:55,690
Nothing touches it, yet the pull goes on.

72
00:02:56,290 --> 00:02:57,428
Now you try.

73
00:02:57,428 --> 00:03:00,325
Air pushing back on a falling raindrop.

74
00:03:00,325 --> 00:03:02,017
Contact or non-contact?

75
00:03:02,017 --> 00:03:03,432
Pause and decide.

76
00:03:06,632 --> 00:03:07,532
Contact!

77
00:03:07,322 --> 00:03:11,178
Air molecules hit the surface
of the drop, and push on it.

78
00:03:11,828 --> 00:03:14,667
Non-contact forces do not even need air.

79
00:03:14,667 --> 00:03:19,347
The Sun's gravity holds Earth across
about one hundred and fifty million

80
00:03:19,347 --> 00:03:21,287
kilometres of empty space.

81
00:03:21,687 --> 00:03:22,587
Your turn.

82
00:03:22,530 --> 00:03:27,484
In empty space, with no air at all,
would a magnet still pull a steel pin?

83
00:03:27,484 --> 00:03:28,725
Pause and decide.

84
00:03:31,925 --> 00:03:32,825
Yes!

85
00:03:32,474 --> 00:03:34,908
A magnetic force needs no medium.

86
00:03:34,908 --> 00:03:37,945
Like gravity, it acts across empty space.

87
00:03:38,595 --> 00:03:40,330
Now, one worked example.

88
00:03:40,680 --> 00:03:44,968
A fifty gram magnet sticks to a steel
cupboard door, which pulls it in

89
00:03:44,968 --> 00:03:46,286
with three newtons.

90
00:03:46,286 --> 00:03:47,910
List every force on it.

91
00:03:48,410 --> 00:03:50,878
Gravity, from Earth, non-contact.

92
00:03:50,878 --> 00:03:56,041
Zero point zero five times nine point
eight is zero point four nine newtons,

93
00:03:56,041 --> 00:03:56,941
down.

94
00:03:57,394 --> 00:04:00,419
The magnetic pull, from
the door, non-contact.

95
00:04:00,419 --> 00:04:02,000
Three newtons, inward.

96
00:04:02,750 --> 00:04:05,603
The door pushes back out, a contact force.

97
00:04:05,603 --> 00:04:09,361
The magnet stays put, so this
push is also three newtons.

98
00:04:10,111 --> 00:04:14,746
And friction from the door, a contact
force, holds it up with zero point

99
00:04:14,746 --> 00:04:16,060
four nine newtons.

100
00:04:16,060 --> 00:04:18,027
Two contact, two non-contact.

101
00:04:18,677 --> 00:04:20,142
Now, the exam trap.

102
00:04:20,492 --> 00:04:24,707
Many students say a ball thrown up
still carries an upward force of the

103
00:04:24,707 --> 00:04:25,607
throw.

104
00:04:25,687 --> 00:04:27,853
But the push is a contact force.

105
00:04:27,853 --> 00:04:30,363
It ends when the ball leaves the hand.

106
00:04:30,363 --> 00:04:33,656
In the air, only gravity
acts, if we ignore air.

107
00:04:34,306 --> 00:04:35,712
Where is this asked?

108
00:04:35,712 --> 00:04:39,890
In naming the forces on a body,
and drawing its free body diagram.

109
00:04:40,490 --> 00:04:41,701
Exam level now.

110
00:04:41,701 --> 00:04:45,189
A point two kilogram iron
ball hangs from a thread.

111
00:04:45,189 --> 00:04:49,519
A magnet beside it pulls it sideways
with one point five newtons.

112
00:04:49,519 --> 00:04:50,422
g is ten.

113
00:04:50,422 --> 00:04:51,735
Find the tension.

114
00:04:51,735 --> 00:04:52,997
Pause and solve.

115
00:04:58,397 --> 00:04:59,821
The answer is B.

116
00:04:59,821 --> 00:05:01,632
Two point five newtons.

117
00:05:02,132 --> 00:05:04,338
The weight is point two times ten.

118
00:05:04,338 --> 00:05:05,669
Two newtons, down.

119
00:05:06,169 --> 00:05:08,143
The thread must balance both.

120
00:05:08,143 --> 00:05:11,231
Two newtons down, and
one point five sideways.

121
00:05:11,731 --> 00:05:15,968
So T is the root of two squared
plus one point five squared.

122
00:05:15,968 --> 00:05:17,691
Two point five newtons.

123
00:05:18,291 --> 00:05:19,755
Here is your recap.

124
00:05:19,755 --> 00:05:21,260
Screenshot this card.

125
00:05:24,460 --> 00:05:26,390
Can you answer these without help?

126
00:05:26,790 --> 00:05:27,690
One.

127
00:05:27,286 --> 00:05:30,591
Why does the paper jump
before the comb touches it?

128
00:05:31,491 --> 00:05:32,391
Two.

129
00:05:32,067 --> 00:05:36,777
Is the force of air on a falling
raindrop contact or non-contact?

130
00:05:37,677 --> 00:05:38,577
Three.

131
00:05:38,252 --> 00:05:42,442
Which forces act on a ball in the
air, after it leaves your hand?

132
00:05:43,542 --> 00:05:46,328
Next time: a table pushes up on your book.

133
00:05:46,328 --> 00:05:48,593
How does it know how hard to push?

134
00:05:48,593 --> 00:05:49,683
Normal reaction.
